[list]
[*]Fixed: on a ship in space, a colonist could change out of a vacsuit or power armor at a stand indoors, then walk out through vacuum in their work clothes for the job, and be burnt or killed. The usual case is a bill whose ingredients lie outside the ship, or in a sealed room that only open deck leads to. On a map in space, a colonist is no longer dressed for a job that goes through vacuum when the stand's outfit protects them less than what they have on: they do the job in their own gear. Someone already in uniform changes back first. Reported by a player.
[*]A stand whose outfit protects as well or better, such as a vacsuit stand by the airlock, still dresses them, and now wins over a nearer stand that would take the suit off.
[*]A colonist in uniform who is cut off from their stand by vacuum is not sent back across it to change. When their next job crosses vacuum, a message says their own clothes are out of reach, and the same goes for a stand on another map.
[*]Orders run as given, as always, and a message says when one sends a colonist through vacuum in work clothes that protect less than their own. Each colonist gets at most one of these messages an in-game hour.
[*]Unchanged: on a planet nothing changes, and on a ship whose rooms all connect through air nothing changes either. Colonists still walk wherever the game sends them; only what they wear is different. Where a sealed corridor and open deck both lead somewhere and the deck is shorter, a colonist may still cut across it in uniform.
[/list]
